{"id":"V5-E1","name":"169-parameter readiness head on the pooled representation (frozen-trunk probe)","question":"Can a learned readiness head detect an under-specified request better than the deterministic rule?","corpus":"training/readiness/corpus-v3.jsonl","corpus_sha256":"a8e0456a0c80fd45e1e747e34970353a8c29c605b1944ea6109ecf438cd919d5","corpus_rows":647,"corpus_composition":"301 hand-written and 346 model-generated rows, all of them audited blind by a different model family than the one that wrote them; 19 rejected by the audit and 15 more as their pair partners","dataset_sha256":"c7ddc5eeb5fc194b89c9442cf0d7e47ba908dafefd59f0cf9941c47c5e77ae6f","base_checkpoint_sha256":"26450f5997c3b55fc2a6ba095a1bffa97879500fce5eb126b8264342c36db321","probe_checkpoint_sha256":"845a8a749131a7575f96c29fe61a5baa1190888751144716a38da159163433ac","architecture":"aaron-intent-1m-v1r:10240x84h754c14L96","parameter_count":1000169,"parameter_budget":1000000,"head_parameters":169,"threshold_selection":"training split of the readiness corpus","threshold":0.39,"readiness_test_at_the_selected_threshold":{"head":{"false_ready_rate":0.1961,"false_not_ready_rate":0.6364,"precision":0.5942,"recall":0.8039,"f1":0.6833},"rule":{"false_ready_rate":0.6863,"false_not_ready_rate":0.0227,"precision":0.9412,"recall":0.3137,"f1":0.4706}},"readiness_validation_at_the_selected_threshold":{"head":{"false_ready_rate":0.1667,"false_not_ready_rate":0.5273,"precision":0.6081,"recall":0.8333,"f1":0.7031},"rule":{"false_ready_rate":0.7037,"false_not_ready_rate":0.0,"precision":1.0,"recall":0.2963,"f1":0.4571}},"dominance":"on the test split the rule sits at (false-ready 0.6863, false-not-ready 0.0227). There is no threshold at which the head is better on both axes: at 0.45 its false-ready rate is 0.2941 (better) at 21x the false-not-ready rate, and at 0.80 its false-not-ready rate is 0.0000 (better) at a far worse false-ready rate of 0.9608. Strict dominance holds only around 0.60-0.70.","agent_level_dispatch_rate_on_the_safety_benchmark":{"baseline":0.169,"head_at_0.30":0.0282,"head_at_0.50":0.0775,"head_at_0.60":0.1197,"head_at_0.80":0.162,"note":"the promotion gate's limit is 0.02. Reaching it requires a false-not-ready rate of 0.86 or worse; the measured dispatch rate at that operating point (threshold 0.30) is 0.0282, which still fails the 0.02 gate. Refusing none of the complete requests leaves the rate at 0.1620, eight times the limit."},"regression_suite_behaviour":{"baseline":{"acted":0,"asked":0,"refused":23,"answered":19},"head_at_the_selected_threshold":{"acted":0,"asked":7,"refused":17,"answered":18},"note":"seven of forty-two complete regression requests are asked about instead of answered, and four of the policy denials are preempted by a clarification"},"verdict":"REJECTED - the head learns the task well (F1 0.683 against the rule's 0.471 on the test split) but there is no threshold at which it is better than the rule on both axes; the dispatch-rate reduction it buys on adversarial input costs 0.86 of complete requests. It also costs 169 parameters over the budget, and 40% more latency per classification.","note":"The corpus is deliberately biased towards the rule's blind spots, so the rule's absolute error rate on it is inflated relative to real traffic. The comparison is therefore favourable to the head, and the head still loses on the deployed trade-off."}
